   "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
   "text": "Nvidia's CEO Resigns After a Spy Scandal.\nJensen Huang, the co-founder of Nvidia, has resigned as the company's CEO.\nHe led Nvidia for thirty-three years and turned it into the most important maker of AI chips.\nHis exit, announced on November 12, came after a scandal that grew very quickly.\nIt began on October 29,\nwhen The New York Times reported that US intelligence officials suspected a business executive named Alice Huang of working for Chinese intelligence.\nShe had worked for Megaspeed, a company that bought large numbers of Nvidia chips.\nThe two are not related.\nOn November 8, Huang admitted in a letter to employees that he had been unfaithful to his wife.\nHe apologized to his family, to Nvidia's board and to shareholders.\nThe board said it would run an independent investigation,\nbut it also said it had no reason to believe that national security or company secrets had been compromised.\nThe pressure kept growing.\nNews reports described more than one relationship, including one with an executive who reported to him.\nNvidia's stock fell.\nFour days after his letter, Huang stepped down as CEO and left the board, effective immediately.\nNvidia did not give a reason.\nColette Kress, Nvidia's chief financial officer, is now the interim CEO while the board looks for a permanent replacement.\nHarvey Jones became the company's first-ever chair.\nNvidia will report its latest results on November 17, and investors will be watching closely."
