   "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
   "text": "Why Jensen Huang's Fall Matters Beyond Nvidia.\nFew executives have been as closely tied to a company as Jensen Huang was to Nvidia.\nHe co-founded the chipmaker in 1993 and ran it for thirty-three years,\nsteering it from a maker of gaming graphics cards to the backbone of the AI boom,\nworth more than four trillion dollars.\nOn November 12, he resigned as chief executive and left the board with immediate effect.\nThe trigger was a story that mixed private conduct with national security.\nIn late October, The New York Times reported that US intelligence suspected Alice Huang, a former executive at Megaspeed,\nof having been recruited by China's Ministry of State Security.\nMegaspeed, a data-center company, had bought billions of dollars' worth of Nvidia chips.\nHuang later admitted to an extramarital relationship with her in a letter to staff, saying he was deeply sorry.\nNvidia's board initially stood behind him.\nIt launched an independent investigation but stated that it had no reason to believe national security or company secrets had been compromised.\nWithin days, however, reports of another relationship, with an executive who reported to him,\nand a falling share price changed the calculation.\nNvidia's announcement gave no reason for his departure.\nThe consequences reach well beyond one company.\nHuang had been the industry's most influential voice against strict US limits on selling advanced chips to China.\nAnalysts say the scandal weakens that argument at a sensitive moment and could encourage lawmakers to push for tighter export controls.\nA House committee chair has also said that Huang is willing to testify before Congress.\nFor now, chief financial officer Colette Kress is serving as interim CEO while keeping her finance role,\nand Harvey Jones has become the board's first-ever chair.\nThe first real test comes on November 17, when Nvidia reports its quarterly results.\nInvestors will be listening not only to the numbers,\nbut to how confidently the company describes its future without its founder."
  }
